{"qid":"Q1007","names":["Republic of Guinea-Bissau"],"title":"Republic of Guinea-Bissau","endonym":{"native":"República da Guiné-Bissau","lang":"pt"},"capsule":"Guinea-Bissau declared independence from Portugal on 24 September 1973, following the eleven-year armed liberation struggle led by the PAIGC under Amílcar Cabral, and independence was recognized by Portugal in September 1974 after the Carnation Revolution. The country's post-independence history has been among Africa's most unstable: military coups in 1980, 1999, 2003, 2009, and 2012 repeatedly interrupted civilian rule. Its economy centers on cashew-nut exports, which make Guinea-Bissau one of the world's largest cashew producers, though the country remains heavily aid-dependent.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Guinea-Bissau's borders were inherited from Portuguese colonial administration and are precisely defined; its post-independence political history is thoroughly documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Guinea-Bissau","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guinea-Bissau"},{"label":"Britannica: Guinea-Bissau","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Guinea-Bissau"},{"label":"Britannica: Amílcar Cabral","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Amilcar-Cabral"}],"britannica":"place/Guinea-Bissau","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Guinea-Bissau","rulers":[{"qid":"Q1389769","name":"Francisco Mendes","start":1973,"end":1978,"image":"Chico Mendes 1973.tiff","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Chico%20Mendes%201973.tiff?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","artist":"Roel Coutinho","attributio